<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amor De Cosmos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Alexander Smith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amor De Cosmos, born William Alexander Smith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, was a journalist and politician in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>. He founded the newspaper British Colonist in Victoria in 1858 and campaigned for responsible government and for British Columbia to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. After the colony entered Confederation in 1871 he served as the province's second premier from 1872 to 1874 and later as a member of the federal parliament.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amor-de-cosmos.jpg" alt="Amor De Cosmos" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amor_de_Cosmos_2a.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Alexander Smith was born on 20 August 1825 in Windsor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, to United Empire Loyalist parents. After studying at King's College in Windsor he became a clerk in Halifax around 1840, where he joined the Dalhousie University debating club and came under the influence of the reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-howe">Joseph Howe</a>. In 1852 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, ran a daguerreotype studio in Kanesville, Iowa, and then joined the California Gold Rush, photographing miners at Placerville. In 1854 the California State Assembly granted his petition to change his name to Amor De Cosmos, which he explained as love of order, beauty, the world and the universe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1858 he moved to Victoria, then booming as the starting point for the Fraser Canyon Gold Rush, and founded The Daily British Colonist, which he edited until 1863. A liberal reformer, he attacked the family-company compact of Hudson's Bay men around Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-douglas-governor">Sir James Douglas</a> and argued for free enterprise, public schools and an elected assembly, as well as for farming, forestry and fisheries as the base of the economy.</p><p>He sat in the assembly of Vancouver Island from 1863 to 1866 and in that of the united colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a>, and led the Confederation League with Robert Beaven and John Robson, earning the name of British Columbia's Father of Confederation. Passed over in 1871 for John Foster McCreight, he became premier on 23 December 1872. He resigned over the Texada Scandal, which arose from his part in a venture to build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> industry on iron from Texada Island, though a Royal Commission found him not guilty. As a Liberal member of the House of Commons for Victoria he pressed for the railway and opposed land concessions to First Nations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>De Cosmos lost his seat in the 1882 federal election and retired to Victoria. Known as an orator, he was also seen as eccentric, never married, had a fear of electricity and grew increasingly incoherent. He was declared insane by 1895 and died in Victoria on 4 July 1897, aged 71. The newspaper he founded survives as the Victoria Times-Colonist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amor De Cosmos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Alaska Purchase of 1867, some residents favoured annexation to the United States, while others, led by the journalist <b>Amor De Cosmos</b>, campaigned to join Canada.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871">Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Windsor, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in Victoria, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.98,-64.13&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Windsor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Amor De Cosmos"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4750 3729h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4750 3729h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.98,-64.13&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
